import CompactLieHeatKernel.AnalyticBridge

/-!
# Literature axiom: the heat kernel of a compact connected Lie group

**This file contains an axiom.  It is imported temporarily from the literature because the result
is classical; it is not proved in Lean.**  The root module `CompactLieHeatKernel` does not import
this file, so `import CompactLieHeatKernel` stays axiom-free.  Every declaration that depends on
the axiom shows `CompactLieHeatKernel.Literature.nonempty_heatSemigroupKernelBridge` under
`#print axioms`.  The ledger is `LITERATURE_AXIOMS.md` at the library root.

## Statement

Let `G` be a compact connected Lie group, let the inner product on its Lie algebra be
`Ad`-invariant, and let `μ` be the normalised Haar measure.  Then there is a
`HeatSemigroupKernelBridge`:

* a strongly continuous, self-adjoint, contractive Markov semigroup `P_t` on `L²(G, μ)` whose
  generator is self-adjoint, nonpositive, and agrees with `½Δ_G` on smooth functions
  (`LaplacianGeneratorBridge`);
* for every `t > 0` a jointly smooth, strictly positive, symmetric density `k_t(x, y)` of mass one
  that satisfies Chapman–Kolmogorov and is invariant under left and right translations
  (`GroupHeatKernelData`);
* `P_t f = ∫ k_t(·, y) f(y) dμ(y)` for continuous `f` and `t > 0`.

## Why an axiom is needed at this level

The kernel data alone would be worthless as an axiom: the constant density `1` satisfies every
field of `GroupHeatKernelData`, so its existence is provable.  The content is the identification of
the generator with `½Δ_G`, which only the bridge carries.

## Why the hypotheses are exactly the classical ones

An axiom with a false instance would make Lean inconsistent, so each hypothesis beyond those needed
to state the bridge is load-bearing:

* `[ConnectedSpace G]`: strict positivity of the density fails on a disconnected group.
* `IsAdInvariantInnerProduct`: right invariance of the density needs a bi-invariant metric.
* `[I.Boundaryless]`: excludes boundary models, where manifold derivatives are taken within a
  half-space.
* `[BorelSpace G]`: with a coarser σ-algebra the measurability field could force a constant
  density.
* `μ` is a left- and right-invariant probability measure on a compact metrizable group, hence the
  normalised Haar measure, which is the normalised Riemannian volume of the invariant metric.

The formal `leftLaplacian` is `∑ᵢ Xᵢ Xᵢ f` over an orthonormal basis of left-invariant vector
fields.  On a unimodular group, in particular a compact one, this is the Laplace–Beltrami operator
of the left-invariant metric (Liao 2018, Theorem 3.21 and Proposition 3.22, p. 95).  The
normalised Haar measure is a constant multiple of the Riemannian measure, so the weighted
Laplacian of Grigor'yan (§3.6) is this operator.  All kernel fields are stated for `t ≠ 0` only.

## References

Page numbers are those printed in the books.  All entries marked "checked" were compared with the
text on 2026-09-17.

* A. Grigor'yan, *Heat Kernel and Analysis on Manifolds*, AMS/IP Studies in Advanced Mathematics
  47, American Mathematical Society, 2009.  Checked:
  - Definition 3.17 and §3.6, pp. 67–68: on a weighted manifold `(M, g, μ)` the Laplacian is
    `Δ_μ = div_μ ∇`; for `μ` a constant multiple of the Riemannian measure, as the normalised Haar
    measure is, `Δ_μ` is the Laplace–Beltrami operator;
  - Theorem 4.6, p. 107: the Dirichlet Laplace operator `L = −Δ_μ|W₀²` is self-adjoint and
    nonnegative;
  - Theorem 4.9, p. 115: `P_t = e^{−tL}` is a strongly continuous contraction semigroup of
    self-adjoint operators with generator `L`;
  - Theorem 5.11, p. 133: `f ≥ 0 ⇒ P_t f ≥ 0` and `f ≤ 1 ⇒ P_t f ≤ 1`;
  - Theorem 7.13, p. 198: the heat kernel `p_t(x, y)` exists, is symmetric and nonnegative,
    represents `P_t` on `L²` (7.49), and satisfies the semigroup identity (7.51);
  - Theorem 7.20, p. 208: `p_t(x, y)` is `C^∞` jointly in `t > 0` and `x, y`;
  - Corollary 8.12, p. 229: on a connected manifold `p_t(x, y) > 0` for all `t > 0`;
  - Exercise 8.7, p. 233, and Theorem 11.8, p. 303, with (11.23), p. 304: a compact manifold is
    stochastically complete, `∫ p_t(x, y) dμ(y) = 1`, hence `P_t 1 = 1`;
  - Theorem 9.12, p. 250: the heat kernel is invariant under isometries of the weighted manifold;
    left translations, and for a bi-invariant metric right translations, are such isometries
    since Haar measure is bi-invariant;
  - Theorem 11.5, p. 301: on a complete (for instance compact) manifold `Δ_μ` on smooth compactly
    supported functions is essentially self-adjoint, so the self-adjoint generator is unique.
  Grigor'yan's semigroup is generated by `Δ_μ`; the bridge uses `½Δ_G`, whose kernel with respect
  to the normalised Haar measure is `p_{t/2}`.
* M. Liao, *Invariant Markov Processes Under Lie Group Actions*, Springer, 2018,
  doi:10.1007/978-3-319-92324-6.  Checked:
  - §3.5, p. 93: the Laplace–Beltrami operator commutes with isometries, and Riemannian Brownian
    motion under a left-invariant metric is a continuous Lévy process;
  - Theorem 3.21 and Proposition 3.22, p. 95: under the left-invariant metric of an inner product
    on `𝔤`, `Δ_G = ∑ᵢ ξᵢξᵢ − ξ₀`, and `ξ₀ = 0` exactly when `𝔤` is unimodular; compact groups are
    unimodular (p. 95);
  - Proposition 4.11, p. 115: symmetry of a convolution semigroup on a compact Lie group;
  - Theorem 4.17, p. 124, and Remark 5.24, p. 159: the density of a conjugate-invariant
    convolution semigroup with nondegenerate diffusion part, by a character expansion; smooth in
    `(t, g)` for connected `G`.
* T. Lévy, *Yang–Mills measure on compact surfaces*, Mem. Amer. Math. Soc. 166 (2003), no. 790;
  preprint arXiv:math/0101239.  Checked in both; memoir numbering first, preprint (arXiv v1)
  numbering in brackets:
  - §1.4, (1.6)–(1.10), pp. 7–8 [§1.4, (1.1)–(1.3)]: the discrete Yang–Mills measure has density
    `∏_F p_{σ(F)}(h_F)` with respect to Haar measure, where `p_t` is the fundamental solution of
    `(∂_t − ½Δ) p_t = 0` for the bi-invariant metric normalised to total volume 1; `p_t` is stated
    to be positive, central, inversion invariant and of mass one;
  - §1.6, Theorem 1.22, p. 13, and Proposition 1.26, p. 16 [Theorem 1.6.1, p. 11, and
    Proposition 1.6.5]: invariance by subdivision rests on the fact that `(p_t)` is a convolution
    semigroup;
  - Proposition 4.8 [Proposition 4.2.4]: the character expansion
    `p_t = ∑_λ dim λ e^{−c₂(λ) t / 2} χ_λ`.
  Lévy scales the metric to volume 1, whereas here the inner product is arbitrary and only the
  Haar measure is normalised; the two kernels differ by a rescaling of time.
* Background, not relied on above and not yet checked against the text: I. Chavel, *Eigenvalues
  in Riemannian Geometry*, Pure and Applied Mathematics 115, Academic Press, 1984, chapter "The
  heat kernel for compact manifolds" (supplied as DjVu); B. K. Driver, *YM₂: continuum
  expectations, lattice convergence, and lassos*, Comm. Math. Phys. 123 (1989), 575–616.

## Scope

Only existence is assumed here.  The two-sided small-time Gaussian bounds
(`eq:heat-gaussian-slack`) are the separate axioms of
`CompactLieHeatKernel.Literature.GaussianBounds`, quantified over every bridge (Hsu 2002, Theorem 5.3.4; Grigor'yan 2009, Corollary 15.15).  The
small-time parametrix with its derivative expansion (N. Berline, E. Getzler, M. Vergne, *Heat
Kernels and Dirac Operators*, Grundlehren 298, Springer, 1992, Chapter 2;
`eq:scaled-heat-parametrix`, `eq:differentiated-heat-parametrix`) is not stated: Lean has no
Jacobian of `exp` yet (see `LITERATURE_AXIOMS.md`).
-/

noncomputable section

open MeasureTheory
open scoped Manifold ContDiff

namespace CompactLieHeatKernel.Literature

/-- **Literature axiom, imported temporarily because the result is classical.**

A compact connected Lie group with an `Ad`-invariant inner product on its Lie algebra and its
normalised Haar measure carries the heat semigroup of `½Δ_G` together with its smooth, strictly
positive, bi-invariant heat kernel, packaged as a `HeatSemigroupKernelBridge`.

References (checked against the text): A. Grigor'yan, *Heat Kernel and Analysis on Manifolds*,
AMS/IP Stud. Adv. Math. 47, AMS, 2009, Theorems 4.6, 4.9, 5.11, 7.13, 7.20, 9.12, 11.5, 11.8 and
Corollary 8.12; M. Liao, *Invariant Markov Processes Under Lie Group Actions*, Springer, 2018,
Theorem 3.21 and Proposition 3.22.  The module docstring gives page numbers, what each result
supplies, and why each hypothesis is needed. -/
axiom nonempty_heatSemigroupKernelBridge
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E]
    {H : Type*} [TopologicalSpace H] (I : ModelWithCorners ℝ E H) [I.Boundaryless]
    (G : Type*) [TopologicalSpace G] [ChartedSpace H G] [MeasurableSpace G] [BorelSpace G]
    [CompactSpace G] [ConnectedSpace G] [Group G] [LieGroup I ∞ G]
    (μ : Measure G) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] [μ.IsMulLeftInvariant] [μ.IsMulRightInvariant]
    (hAd : IsAdInvariantInnerProduct (I := I) (G := G)) :
    Nonempty (HeatSemigroupKernelBridge (I := I) (G := G) (μ := μ))

variable
    {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [InnerProductSpace ℝ E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E]
    {H : Type*} [TopologicalSpace H] (I : ModelWithCorners ℝ E H) [I.Boundaryless]
    (G : Type*) [TopologicalSpace G] [ChartedSpace H G] [MeasurableSpace G] [BorelSpace G]
    [CompactSpace G] [ConnectedSpace G] [Group G] [LieGroup I ∞ G]
    (μ : Measure G) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ] [μ.IsMulLeftInvariant] [μ.IsMulRightInvariant]

/-- The heat-kernel bridge of a compact connected Lie group, chosen from the literature axiom
`nonempty_heatSemigroupKernelBridge`.

Lean does not know that the bridge is unique, although it is (Grigor'yan 2009, Theorem 11.5, gives
uniqueness of the self-adjoint generator).  Prove results for an arbitrary
`HeatSemigroupKernelBridge` where possible, and use this choice only to state the final
unconditional form. -/
def heatKernelBridge (hAd : IsAdInvariantInnerProduct (I := I) (G := G)) :
    HeatSemigroupKernelBridge (I := I) (G := G) (μ := μ) :=
  Classical.choice (nonempty_heatSemigroupKernelBridge I G μ hAd) -- totalisation-audit: allow choice from a Nonempty literature axiom; no junk branch

/-- The heat kernel of `½Δ_G`, from the literature axiom. -/
def heatKernel (hAd : IsAdInvariantInnerProduct (I := I) (G := G)) : GroupHeatKernelData I G μ :=
  (heatKernelBridge I G μ hAd).kernel

end CompactLieHeatKernel.Literature
